---
// Law-enforcement and official data requests — example-org/operations#11.
//
// The page exists to answer most requests before they are sent. Its spine is
// the "what we never have" list from the privacy policy, which is in turn the
// Art. 30 record (`operations/legal/vvt.md` §5) — the same inventory, stated to
// a different reader. If those three ever disagree, this page is the one most
// likely to be read under time pressure by someone with a legal instrument,
// so it is the one that must not drift.
//
// It states law, and therefore it states law carefully: what a provision
// obliges, and what it does not. Overstating our obligations would invite
// requests we cannot answer; understating them would be worse.
//
// German version: `behoerdenanfragen.astro`. Same document.
import LandingLayout from "../layouts/LandingLayout.astro";
import GlassNav from "../components/GlassNav.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { absoluteOg } from "../lib/og";

const title = "Law Enforcement Requests — Myne";
const description =
  "What Myne can and cannot produce in response to an official request: an account hash, a login verifier, counters and ciphertext we hold no key to. No email, no name, no IP address, no readable content.";
const canonical = new URL("/law-enforcement/", Astro.site!).toString();
const ogImage = absoluteOg("law-enforcement", Astro.site!);

const eyebrow = "font-size:11px;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:14px;";
const h2 =
  "font-size:clamp(19px,2.2vw,25px);font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-0.02em;line-height:1.15;margin-top:56px;";
const h3 =
  "font-size:16px;font-weight:650;letter-spacing:-0.01em;margin-top:32px;color:var(--ink-1);";
const body =
  "margin-top:14px;max-width:68ch;font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink-3);";
const cardStyle =
  "border:1px solid rgb(var(--line-rgb) / 0.1);border-radius:12px;padding:22px;background:var(--paper-2);margin-top:22px;";
const tableWrap = "margin-top:22px;overflow-x:auto;";
const table =
  "width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;min-width:560px;";
const th =
  "text-align:left;padding:10px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgb(var(--line-rgb) / 0.14);color:var(--ink-1);font-weight:650;white-space:nowrap;";
const td =
  "padding:10px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgb(var(--line-rgb) / 0.06);color:var(--ink-3);vertical-align:top;";
const tdEm = td + "color:var(--ink-1);white-space:nowrap;";

const have = [
  ["account_number_hash", "A 16-byte hash. Not the account number itself."],
  [
    "SCRAM verifier",
    "What a login is checked against. Not a password, and not reversible into one.",
  ],
  [
    "Encrypted blobs",
    "Notes and attachments as ciphertext. We hold no key. Producible only in that form.",
  ],
  [
    "Device registry",
    "One record per enrolled device: a client-generated device id and enrolment/activity state. No device names, no hardware identifiers, no addresses.",
  ],
  ["Counters", "Total bytes stored, number of devices."],
  [
    "Account policy",
    "A freeze flag and any per-account storage or device limit. Server-side plain text.",
  ],
];

const haveNot = [
  "Email address — none is collected at signup, and there is no field for one",
  "Name, postal address, telephone number",
  "Payment details — the service takes none",
  "IP addresses — not logged, in any persisted form, at any layer",
  "Connection logs, access times, session history",
  "Readable content — note text, titles, folder names, tags",
  "Timestamps that mean anything — they exist only inside the ciphertext",
];
---

<LandingLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogImage={ogImage}
  notice="draft"
  robots="noindex,nofollow"
>
  <div
    style="font-family:Inter,ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif;color:var(--ink-1);background:var(--paper);min-height:100vh;"
  >
    <GlassNav active="legal" />

    <section id="top" style="padding:128px 28px 70px;">
      <div style="max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;">
        <div class="mono up" style={eyebrow}>Legal</div>
        <h1
          style="font-size:clamp(30px,5vw,50px);font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-0.03em;line-height:1.05;"
        >
          Law Enforcement Requests
        </h1>
        <p style={body}>
          This page is for authorities and their counsel. It sets out what we hold, what we do not,
          and what a request can therefore produce — so that a request can be framed against what
          exists rather than against what a notes service is usually assumed to have.
        </p>

        <div style={cardStyle}>
          <div class="mono up" style={eyebrow}>Where to write</div>
          <p style="margin:0;font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--ink-3);">
            <a href="mailto:legal@myne.md" style="color:var(--accent);">legal@myne.md</a> — in German or English.
            Requests are accepted electronically; there is no requirement to post paper. Please include
            the legal basis you are relying on and the account identifier you have.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 style={h2}>What we can produce</h2>
        <p style={body}>
          Myne is end-to-end encrypted: content is encrypted on the user's device with keys derived
          from a password the user holds, and those keys never reach the server. What remains on our
          side is the following, in full:
        </p>

        <div style={tableWrap}>
          <table style={table}>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th style={th}>What</th>
                <th style={th}>What it is</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                have.map(([what, desc]) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td style={tdEm}>{what}</td>
                    <td style={td}>{desc}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 style={h2}>What does not exist</h2>
        <p style={body}>
          These are not withheld — they are absent. No process, court order or technical measure on
          our side can produce them, because nothing generates them in the first place:
        </p>
        <ul style={body + "padding-left:20px;"}>
          {haveNot.map((x) => <li style="margin-top:8px;">{x}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p style={body}>
          Several of these are consequences of the architecture rather than of policy, and cannot be
          reversed by a change of mind on our part. Registration collects no email because the flow
          has no such step. Rate limiting operates on per-account counters because the server does
          not retain the address a request arrived from. A user's own timestamps sit inside the
          encrypted blob, so no operator can read when anything was written.
        </p>

        <h2 style={h2}>Legal basis, and what each one reaches</h2>

        <h3 style={h3}>§ 22 TDDDG — subscriber data</h3>
        <p style={body}>
          A request under § 22 TDDDG must be made in writing or electronically. In a criminal
          investigation it requires sufficient factual grounds; a court order is not a precondition
          of this provision. Where the requested data exists, it is transmitted without undue delay
          and in full.
        </p>
        <p style={body}>
          <strong style="color:var(--ink-1);">§ 22 does not create a duty to collect.</strong> It governs
          disclosure of subscriber data a provider holds. It does not require a provider to begin holding
          data it does not hold, and it cannot reach data that was never generated. In practice this means
          a § 22 request against a Myne account produces the account hash, the device registry, the counters
          and the policy flags — and nothing resembling an identity, because none is on file.
        </p>

        <h3 style={h3}>§ 23 TDDDG — passwords and access credentials</h3>
        <p style={body}>
          § 23 reaches access credentials, and only for the serious offences catalogued in § 100b
          StPO, on a court order. Two things bound what it can produce here. First, we hold no
          password: what is stored is a SCRAM verifier, which authenticates a login and cannot be
          turned back into the credential that satisfies it. Second, the provision itself leaves
          encryption untouched — <em>eine Verschlüsselung der Daten bleibt unberührt</em>. An order
          under § 23 does not become an order to decrypt.
        </p>

        <h3 style={h3}>Decryption</h3>
        <p style={body}>
          There is no basis in German law that obliges a provider to break the encryption of its own
          service, and in this case the question is moot before it is legal: the keys are derived on
          the user's device from a secret the user holds, and no copy reaches us. We cannot decrypt
          the data on request, on order, or of our own volition.
        </p>
        <p style={body}>
          Encrypted data is therefore produced in encrypted form. If an order requires production,
          what we can hand over is the ciphertext exactly as it sits on disk.
        </p>

        <h2 style={h2}>How we handle a request</h2>
        <p style={body}>
          Each request is reviewed against the applicable provision and answered on its terms. Where
          a request seeks data that exists, it is produced. Where it seeks data that does not exist,
          we say so plainly and explain why — this page is the long form of that answer.
        </p>
        <p style={body}>
          We notify the affected user where we are permitted to do so and where a channel exists to
          reach them. Both conditions matter here: with no email address on file, there is often no
          channel at all, which is a consequence of collecting nothing rather than a decision to
          stay silent.
        </p>
        <p style={body}>
          Emergency requests involving a risk to life or physical safety are handled with priority.
          Write to the address above and say so in the subject line.
        </p>

        <h2 style={h2}>Self-hosted deployments</h2>
        <p style={body}>
          Myne can be self-hosted, and the server software is open source. Where an account lives on
          someone else's server, we are not the provider and hold nothing at all — not the ciphertext,
          not the account hash, not the fact that the account exists. Requests about such accounts
          have to reach the operator of that deployment.
        </p>

        <p style={body + "margin-top:44px;font-size:14px;color:var(--ink-3);"}>
          Deutsche Fassung: <a href="/behoerdenanfragen/" style="color:var(--accent);">Behördenanfragen</a
          >. See also the <a href="/privacy/" style="color:var(--accent);">Privacy Policy</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <SiteFooter />
  </div>
</LandingLayout>
